WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.010
Davao, in the Philippines.

2
00:00:03.130 --> 00:00:06.360
Sixty-four farmers grow
cacao for one co-op.

3
00:00:07.210 --> 00:00:10.140
Every delivery is weighed,
and written on a paper slip.

4
00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:15.900
Then a chocolate maker overseas pays
one premium, for the whole harvest.

5
00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:18.500
One payment.

6
00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:20.590
Sixty-four farmers.

7
00:00:21.670 --> 00:00:24.940
The split is done by hand,
from a box of slips.

8
00:00:25.790 --> 00:00:27.850
A carbon copy gets counted twice.

9
00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:31.240
A fine appears that no rule allows.

10
00:00:32.270 --> 00:00:33.880
Farmers can't check their share.

11
00:00:34.810 --> 00:00:37.190
The buyer can't prove the money arrived.

12
00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:42.610
Fair Split turns a season of
paper into pay anyone can check.

13
00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:47.850
The vision: every co-op,
every farmer, paid fairly,

14
00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:51.330
by PayPal or in cash,
with proof for the buyer.

15
00:00:52.270 --> 00:00:53.440
Here's what works today.

16
00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:57.760
The buyer pays one PayPal invoice.

17
00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:00.870
Claude reads every slip, twice.

18
00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:04.310
If the readings disagree,
a person decides.

19
00:01:05.510 --> 00:01:11.400
Code, not AI, works out every share, to
the cent, and stops what it can't explain.

20
00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:15.940
This slip was counted twice:
once from its pink copy.

21
00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:20.860
Once fixed, almost eighteen dollars
goes back to sixty-three farmers.

22
00:01:22.070 --> 00:01:24.160
Two officers sign the same batch:

23
00:01:24.630 --> 00:01:27.480
the treasurer at her desk,
the chair on his phone.

24
00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:30.750
PayPal pays the farmers
who have an account.

25
00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:33.690
The rest get cash, on the same statement.

26
00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:38.230
Nang Celing reads her pay in
her own language, or listens.

27
00:01:39.310 --> 00:01:41.780
One tap shows the slip behind each peso.

28
00:01:42.890 --> 00:01:45.990
And the buyer checks every
line, in her own browser.

29
00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:52.600
Before: a box of slips, and trust.

30
00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:56.550
Now: one pay run that explains itself.

31
00:01:57.190 --> 00:01:58.900
In our demo, under a minute.

32
00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:02.390
Under the hood, four rules.

33
00:02:03.221 --> 00:02:05.441
One: AI only reads.

34
00:02:06.070 --> 00:02:09.340
Claude turns slips into data,
and never touches a number.

35
00:02:10.150 --> 00:02:14.490
Two: code does the math, under
rules sealed before the season.

36
00:02:15.241 --> 00:02:16.951
Three: people approve.

37
00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:20.370
Two signatures lock one
fingerprint of the batch.

38
00:02:21.190 --> 00:02:23.330
Four: PayPal moves the money.

39
00:02:24.130 --> 00:02:28.230
The invoice goes through PayPal's Agent
Toolkit; payouts through our own tool,

40
00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:31.630
sent exactly once, and
confirmed by signed notices.

41
00:02:32.530 --> 00:02:35.350
Every step is chained into
a tamper-evident record.

42
00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:40.730
Fair Split is a hackathon MVP:

43
00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:44.770
PayPal runs on our simulator,
and the co-op is synthetic.

44
00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:47.320
The vision is real.

45
00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:51.820
Fair Split. Every peso, traced.
